/-!
# [M:Mᵖ]=p for a one-variable function field over a perfect field

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.finrank_frobeniusSubfield_eq_of_transcendental`
(`4399ba35-43e6-5b5d-8ef1-d153ef817153`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `bb6ff04e-78e4-5201-a7c3-39610405eac0`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $M$ be fields with $M$ a $K$-algebra, let $K$ be perfect, and let $p$ be a prime with
$K$ of characteristic $p$. Let $t \in M$ be transcendental over $K$, i.e. no nonzero polynomial over
$K$ vanishes at $t$, and assume that $M$ is finite-dimensional as a module over the intermediate
field $K(t) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {t}` generated by $t$. Since the structure map $K \to M$
is injective, $M$ also has exponential characteristic $p$, and the Frobenius endomorphism $x \mapsto
x^p$ of $M$ is available; its field range is the subfield $M^p$ of $p$-th powers. The assertion is
that the rank of $M$ as a module over this subfield is exactly $p$, that is $[M : M^p] = p$. (The
rank is the `Module.finrank` of $M$ over the coercion of `(frobenius M p).fieldRange`, so the
equality with $p$ in particular records that this rank is finite.)

This is the classical computation of the degree of a one-variable algebraic function field over its
subfield of $p$-th powers, for a perfect constant field of characteristic $p$; both hypotheses
matter, since imperfect constant fields or higher transcendence degree give larger indices. Within
the development it underlies the computation of degrees of Frobenius-type endomorphisms and the
existence of separating transcendental elements, and is used in the analysis of norm formulae for
Weierstrass curves over algebraically closed fields of positive characteristic.

